{"parentUuid":"9a1cbd2e-877a-4917-afd6-b11a0575c5e7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GKJPMungZrP8H78SgQHY9J","type":"tool_result","content":"# MergeSat's Transparent Huge Pages Implementation\n\nBased on the provided documentation, here's what's reported about THP usage in MergeSat:\n\n**Reported Speedup:**\nThe documentation states: \"This typically results in a 10% speedup (average), but also can result in less reproducability of runtime, as transparent huge pages might not be available.\"\n\n**How THP is Enabled:**\nThe mechanism is implemented through the Docker build process. According to the README, \"the binary created with the docker file tries to back allocated memory with transparent huge pages.\"\n\n**Kernel Parameters:**\nThe documentation provided contains no specific information about kernel parameters, tuning commands, or configuration details needed to enable THP. It only mentions that THP \"might not be available\" depending on the system, implying it relies on kernel support rather than explicit solver-level configuration.\n\nThe approach appears to leverage the Docker containerization environment rather than manual kernel parameter adjustments in the solver itself."}]},"uuid":"7dea2c3c-c5e6-4dea-968a-d9c99afc74b4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:42.311Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9a1cbd2e-877a-4917-afd6-b11a0575c5e7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3f6d5290-83a6-4d06-b9b8-1f77e03c01ee","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UZVMrAPD57ptWK11tDdjmX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Intel 13900KS P-core E-core Linux scheduler EEVDF taskset numactl\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[Solved] Can I tell a program to not use specific cpu cores? / Newbie Corner / Arch Linux Forums\",\"url\":\"https://bbs.archlinux.org/viewtopic.php?id=271838\"},{\"title\":\"How do I change CPU scheduler? - Linux - Level1Techs Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forum.level1techs.com/t/how-do-i-change-cpu-scheduler/201662\"},{\"title\":\"Managing Process Affinity in Linux\",\"url\":\"https://glennklockwood.com/hpc-howtos/process-affinity.html\"},{\"title\":\"Processor and Memory Affinity Tools » ADMIN Magazine\",\"url\":\"https://www.admin-magazine.com/HPC/Articles/Processor-and-Memory-Affinity-Tools\"},{\"title\":\"Take control of your Intel CPU's P-Cores and E-Cores with CoreDirector software | Tom's Hardware\",\"url\":\"https://www.tomshardware.com/news/bitsun-coredirector-intel-cpu-e-cores\"},{\"title\":\"6.3. Configuration Suggestions | Performance Tuning Guide | Red Hat Enterprise Linux | 7 | Red Hat Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/7/html/performance_tuning_guide/sect-red_hat_enterprise_linux-performance_tuning_guide-cpu-configuration_suggestions\"},{\"title\":\"The current state of Alder Lake E-cores and P-cores on Linux - Linux - Level1Techs Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forum.level1techs.com/t/the-current-state-of-alder-lake-e-cores-and-p-cores-on-linux/199603\"},{\"title\":\"Notes on running Redis with HPC techniques · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/neomantra/3c9b89887d19be6fa5708bf4017c0ecd\"},{\"title\":\"EEVDF Scheduler Merged For Linux 6.6, Intel Hybrid Cluster Scheduling Re-Introduced - Phoronix Forums\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/forums/forum/software/general-linux-open-source/1406196-eevdf-scheduler-merged-for-linux-6-6-intel-hybrid-cluster-scheduling-re-introduced/page2\"},{\"title\":\"A.15. taskset | Performance Tuning Guide | Red Hat Enterprise Linux | 7 | Red Hat Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/7/html/performance_tuning_guide/sect-red_hat_enterprise_linux-performance_tuning_guide-tool_reference-taskset\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found related to your query:\n\n## Linux Scheduler and Process Affinity Tools\n\nnumactl and taskset can bind a process to a list of cores. These are the primary tools for managing CPU affinity on Linux systems.\n\n### taskset vs numactl\n\nThe emerging standard for easily binding processes to processors on Linux-based supercomputers is numactl. It can operate on a coarser-grained basis (i.e., CPU sockets rather than individual CPU cores) than taskset (only CPU cores) because it is aware of the processor topology. Additionally, taskset does not guarantee local memory allocation. If you require the additional performance benefits of local memory allocation, Red Hat recommends using numactl instead of taskset.\n\n### EEVDF Scheduler\n\nRegarding the EEVDF scheduler mentioned in your query, EEVDF is an objectively better scheduler due to using less heuristics, and EEVDF has been existing for years now, with the patchset being very mature at this point. The search results also note that EEVDF may show benefits most likely on systems with hybrid CPU designs (big.Little|E-Core/P-Core).\n\n### P-Cores and E-Cores on Linux\n\nWhile the search results discuss hybrid core architectures, they don't contain specific technical details about the Intel 13900KS's P-core and E-core scheduling with EEVDF, taskset, and numactl interactions. For more detailed technical information on this specific topic, you may want to search technical forums or kernel documentation focused on hybrid CPU scheduling.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"481798fa-7d48-41ad-bcc1-7f9758fe713c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:45.243Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3f6d5290-83a6-4d06-b9b8-1f77e03c01ee","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9a7bf79e-dbb7-4256-8e9f-95223e3da333","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QhN63H9i3v1rb4j4GVLi2q","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cgroups v2 memory.high memory.max OOM protect process Linux\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Diagnosing Linux cgroups v2 Memory Throttling & OOM-Killed Containers | Netdata\",\"url\":\"https://www.netdata.cloud/academy/diagnosing-linux-cgroups/\"},{\"title\":\"Memory Controller · cgroup2\",\"url\":\"https://facebookmicrosites.github.io/cgroup2/docs/memory-controller.html\"},{\"title\":\"Control Group v2 — The Linux Kernel documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html\"},{\"title\":\"Control groups (cgroups) v2\",\"url\":\"https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/cgroup-v2.txt\"},{\"title\":\"How to Configure Memory Limits with Cgroups on Talos Linux\",\"url\":\"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-03-03-configure-memory-limits-with-cgroups-on-talos-linux/view\"},{\"title\":\"kindatechnical() | A Guide to Operating Systems - cgroups v2: Resource Limits, CPU Weight, Memory High/Max, and Pressure\",\"url\":\"https://kindatechnical.com/operating-systems/lesson-98-cgroups-v2.html\"},{\"title\":\"Kubernetes 1.27: Quality-of-Service for Memory Resources (alpha) | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2023/05/05/qos-memory-resources/\"},{\"title\":\"Memory Resource Controller\",\"url\":\"https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/cgroup-v1/memory.txt\"},{\"title\":\"Controlling Process Resources with Linux Control Groups | iximiuz Labs\",\"url\":\"https://labs.iximiuz.com/tutorials/controlling-process-resources-with-cgroups\"},{\"title\":\"How to Use Memory Cgroups to Limit and Monitor Application Memory on RHEL\",\"url\":\"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-03-04-use-memory-cgroups-to-limit-and-monitor-application-memory/view\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's comprehensive information about cgroups v2 memory controls for managing and protecting processes on Linux:\n\n## Key Memory Controls in cgroups v2\n\n### memory.high (Soft Limit - Throttling)\nmemory.high is a memory usage throttle limit where if a cgroup's usage goes over the high boundary, the processes of the cgroup are throttled and put under heavy reclaim pressure. Going over the high limit never invokes the OOM killer and under extreme conditions the limit may be breached.\n\nWhen a cgroup's memory usage exceeds memory.high, the kernel starts to aggressively throttle the processes within that cgroup. The kernel will try to reclaim memory pages from the cgroup to push its usage back below the memory.high mark. This means that when a process inside the container tries to allocate more memory, its execution is paused while the kernel works to free up space.\n\n### memory.max (Hard Limit - OOM Protection)\nIf a process inside the cgroup attempts to allocate memory that would push the group's total usage over memory.max, the kernel's OOM killer is invoked specifically for that cgroup. It will find a process within the cgroup to kill to free up memory.\n\n### Process Protection: memory.min and memory.low\nmemory.min provides hard memory protection where if the memory usage of a cgroup is within its effective min boundary, the cgroup's memory won't be reclaimed under any conditions. memory.min is mapped to requests.memory, which results in reservation of memory resources that should never be reclaimed by the kernel.\n\n## Practical Benefits\n\nBecause breach of the high limit doesn't trigger the OOM killer but throttles the offending cgroup, a management agent has ample opportunities to monitor and take appropriate actions such as granting more memory or terminating the workload.\n\nThe memory controller provides two boundaries: memory.high (soft limit that triggers reclaim and throttling) and memory.max (hard limit that triggers OOM killing). Using both together lets the system apply back-pressure before killing processes.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"00fd65f3-87f4-4d78-a77d-f240647337a6","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:47.861Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9a7bf79e-dbb7-4256-8e9f-95223e3da333","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
