{"parentUuid":"97738280-5cfb-43a5-8fa7-98ee6e324a93","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012WKSVuRhJapM48ff4sjMnP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Towards Faster Reasoners\" transparent huge pages SAT speedup percentage\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[2004.14378] Towards Faster Reasoners By Using Transparent Huge Pages\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.14378\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Faster Reasoners by Using Transparent Huge Pages | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-58475-7_18\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Faster Reasoners by Using Transparent Huge Pages | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344179418_Towards_Faster_Reasoners_by_Using_Transparent_Huge_Pages\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Faster Reasoners By Using Transparent Huge Pages | DeepAI\",\"url\":\"https://deepai.org/publication/towards-faster-reasoners-by-using-transparent-huge-pages\"},{\"title\":\"Julian Stecklina's research works | TUD Dresden University of Technology and other places\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/Julian-Stecklina-2048101806\"},{\"title\":\"Transparent Huge Pages (THP): Reducing TLB Pressure | Abhik Sarkar\",\"url\":\"https://www.abhik.ai/concepts/memory/transparent-huge-pages\"},{\"title\":\"Transparent Hugepage Support — The Linux Kernel documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/transhuge.html\"},{\"title\":\"Transparent Hugepage Support — The Linux Kernel 5.10.0-rc1+ documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.infradead.org/~mchehab/kernel_docs/admin-guide/mm/transhuge.html\"},{\"title\":\"Huge Pages or Transparent Huge Pages in Context of Exadata | exadata\",\"url\":\"https://blogs.oracle.com/exadata/huge-pages-or-transparent-huge-pages-in-context-of-exadata\"},{\"title\":\"Transparent Hugepage Support — The Linux Kernel documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/v5.9/admin-guide/mm/transhuge.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found the paper \"Towards Faster Reasoners By Using Transparent Huge Pages.\" Here are the key findings regarding SAT speedup:\n\nThe paper presents an approach to reduce the runtime of automated reasoning (AR) tools by 10% on average and up to 20% for long running tasks. The experimental results cover tools commonly used in the AR community, including the domains ASP, BMC, MaxSAT, SAT, and SMT.\n\nThe method works by extending the standard C library (glibc) to dynamically allow use of a memory management feature called huge pages, which reduce the overhead required to translate memory addresses between virtual and physical memory.\n\nSo to directly answer your query: The SAT speedup percentage achieved in the \"Towards Faster Reasoners\" paper using transparent huge pages is **10% on average and up to 20% for long running tasks**.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"169bc0f8-930f-4856-bd75-6b03eb4f28eb","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:59.450Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"97738280-5cfb-43a5-8fa7-98ee6e324a93","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"900f75ff-b0c0-4144-91ad-915433d24dd0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WwkMNDsN4teBLtKk6ZvWSj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools CP-SAT solver memory peak RSS Linux benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Memory leak on CP SAT solver · Issue #3212 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3212\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking your Model - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/08_benchmarking.html\"},{\"title\":\"Memory usage while using AddElement constraint\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/enAzF6Cv2vg\"},{\"title\":\"Bottlenecks in CP-SAT Solver Performance Across Core Configurations · Issue #4783 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4783\"},{\"title\":\"Memory Leak · Issue #3083 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3083\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for \"OR-Tools CP-SAT solver memory peak RSS Linux benchmark\". Here's what I found:\n\nThe search results show information about the OR-Tools CP-SAT solver and various discussions related to memory usage, but specific benchmark data on peak RSS memory on Linux is limited in these results.\n\nWhat the results do reveal:\n\n1. **General Information**: The CP-SAT solver is designed for integer programming problems and is generally faster than MPSolver.\n\n2. **Memory Issues Reported**: There are documented cases of significant memory usage. One user reported memory usage of approximately 50GB RAM when using the AddElement constraint for a scheduling problem, and another user experienced consistently increasing memory usage until the process was killed on Linux.\n\n3. **Expected Memory**: Users expected to see consistent memory usage of around 1-2GB, indicating that peak RSS can vary significantly depending on the problem size and constraints used.\n\nThe search results primarily contain discussions and reports from GitHub issues rather than formal benchmark reports with specific peak RSS measurements. For detailed benchmark data on peak RSS memory performance, you may need to check the official OR-Tools documentation or academic papers that specifically benchmark the solver's memory characteristics on Linux systems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5b7703ce-7327-44f6-899f-c1fdc212223f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:01.668Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"900f75ff-b0c0-4144-91ad-915433d24dd0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6fa9c5f6-e0ac-49ef-b790-a529ab32828f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W71rALZjixToEorbU2cmpT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Linux swap zram zswap vs Windows page file performance memory pressure\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"I was wrong! zswap IS better than zram\",\"url\":\"https://linuxblog.io/zswap-better-than-zram/\"},{\"title\":\"Debunking zswap and zram myths - Chris Down\",\"url\":\"https://chrisdown.name/2026/03/24/zswap-vs-zram-when-to-use-what.html\"},{\"title\":\"Swapfile vs Zswap vs ZRAM on VPS in 2025\",\"url\":\"https://onidel.com/blog/swapfile-zswap-zram-vps-2025\"},{\"title\":\"Alan Doyle - Swap vs ZSwap vs zRAM\",\"url\":\"https://alandoyle.com/blog/swap-vs-zswap-vs-zram/\"},{\"title\":\"Linux Performance: Almost Always Add Swap Space - Part 2: ZRAM\",\"url\":\"https://linuxblog.io/linux-performance-almost-always-add-swap-part2-zram/\"},{\"title\":\"Returning to Arch – zram, zswap, swap for Stable Diffusion & Gaming / Installation / Arch Linux Forums\",\"url\":\"https://bbs.archlinux.org/viewtopic.php?id=306068\"},{\"title\":\"zram - ArchWiki\",\"url\":\"https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Zram\"},{\"title\":\"Even if you have 16GB of RAM, this one \\\"compressed swap\\\" trick makes Linux significantly smoother\",\"url\":\"https://www.howtogeek.com/even-if-you-have-16gb-of-ram-this-one-compressed-swap-trick-makes-linux-significantly-smoother/\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Linux Swap Management: How to Use Compressed Swap with Zswap and Zram | by KrisWhite | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@kriswwhite/advanced-linux-swap-management-how-to-use-compressed-swap-with-zswap-and-zram-bc18a0f7a451\"},{\"title\":\"zswap - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zswap\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive information about Linux swap mechanisms. However, the search results focus primarily on Linux (zram/zswap) and don't include detailed comparisons with Windows page file performance. Let me provide what I found:\n\n## Linux Swap Technologies: Zram vs Zswap\n\n**Architectural Differences:**\n\nZswap sits in front of your disk swap, compresses pages in RAM, and automatically tiers cold data to disk, integrating directly with the kernel's memory management and distributing pressure gracefully. In contrast, zram is a compressed RAM block device with a hard capacity limit, and when it fills up, there is no automatic eviction, and the kernel has very little leverage to do anything about the situation.\n\n**Performance Under Memory Pressure:**\n\nZswap degrades significantly better than zram when there is memory pressure. Zswap dynamically compresses and caches hot pages in RAM, evicts cold ones to disk swap, and delivers smoother performance under heavy pressure.\n\n**CPU Overhead:**\n\nZRAM typically consumes 5-15% additional CPU cycles during memory pressure events, while zswap overhead remains lower due to its caching nature.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\nIf your system only uses swap occasionally and keeping swap demand within ~20–30% of your physical RAM as zram is enough, ZRAM is the simpler and more effective option. But if swap use regularly pushes far beyond that, is unpredictable, or if your system has fast storage (NVMe), Zswap is the better choice.\n\n**Note:** The search results don't contain detailed Windows page file performance comparisons. For a comprehensive comparison between Linux and Windows memory management under pressure, you would need additional sources specifically addressing Windows page file behavior.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7c812922-43c0-4092-9285-664e4858dfb2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:03.911Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6fa9c5f6-e0ac-49ef-b790-a529ab32828f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
