{"parentUuid":"cee56428-4112-41e1-a1ad-dc39933790d4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013ZUCz86xWyCm2yAFEDhbUH","type":"tool_result","content":"# Analysis of Quantitative Results in arXiv:2004.14378\n\nBased on the webpage content provided, I cannot extract the specific quantitative details you requested. The page contains only the **abstract and metadata** for the paper, not the full research findings.\n\n## What the Abstract States\n\nThe abstract mentions these aggregate results:\n- \"reduce the runtime of AR tools by 10% on average and up to 20% for long running tasks\"\n- Performance improvements across domains: ASP, BMC, MaxSAT, SAT, and SMT\n\n## Missing Information\n\nThe following details are **not available** on this webpage:\n- Kernel version and hardware specifications\n- Page size parameters\n- Per-solver speedup numbers (CaDiCaL, Glucose, MapleSAT, etc.)\n- PAR-2 score changes\n- TLB miss reduction percentages\n- THP enablement method (madvise vs. always)\n- Detailed long-running task data\n\n## To Access This Data\n\nYou would need to:\n1. Download the full PDF via the \"View PDF\" link\n2. Review the experimental results section in the paper itself\n\nThe webpage only provides the abstract, which omits the granular experimental data you're seeking."}]},"uuid":"d990def2-c3e0-4ce5-a5e3-65e1ed479c56","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:13.716Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cee56428-4112-41e1-a1ad-dc39933790d4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ad0b02eb-0519-4e48-b0c1-25bb4d9f5b0f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GXyEXc98XZ5tp2cftFg7Zt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Linux kernel 6.6 6.8 EEVDF Intel hybrid scheduler P-core preference\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"intel_pstate CPU Performance Scaling Driver — The Linux Kernel documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/intel_pstate.html\"},{\"title\":\"EEVDF Scheduler Merged For Linux 6.6, Intel Hybrid Cluster Scheduling Re-Introduced - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-6.6-EEVDF-Merged\"},{\"title\":\"EEVDF Scheduler Merged For Linux 6.6, Intel Hybrid Cluster Scheduling Re-Introduced - Phoronix Forums\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/forums/forum/phoronix/latest-phoronix-articles/1406196-eevdf-scheduler-merged-for-linux-6-6-intel-hybrid-cluster-scheduling-re-introduced\"},{\"title\":\"Hybrid scheduling gets more complicated [LWN.net]\",\"url\":\"https://lwn.net/Articles/909611/\"},{\"title\":\"Linux_6.18 - Linux Kernel Newbies\",\"url\":\"https://kernelnewbies.org/Linux_6.18\"},{\"title\":\"Linux_6.6 - Linux Kernel Newbies\",\"url\":\"https://kernelnewbies.org/Linux_6.6\"},{\"title\":\"Flexible system software scheduling for asymmetric multicore systems with PMCSched: A case for Intel Alder Lake - Bilbao - 2023 - Concurrency and Computation: Practice and Experience - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/cpe.7814\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key findings about Linux kernel 6.6, 6.8, EEVDF, and Intel hybrid scheduler with P-core preference:\n\n## EEVDF Scheduler in Linux 6.6\n\nThe EEVDF scheduler code has been merged for Linux 6.6 kernel, replacing the existing CFS scheduler code. EEVDF stands for \"Earliest Eligible Virtual Deadline First\" and is based on a research paper from the late 90's. This should result in better scheduling policy for the kernel while also exposing less knobs than CFS due to having better behavior.\n\n## Intel Hybrid Cluster Scheduling\n\nThe scheduler pull also re-introduces cluster scheduling for Intel Core hybrid processors. More specifically, cluster scheduling was enabled for x86 hybrid CPUs in Linux 6.6.\n\n## P-Core Preference in Intel Hybrid Systems\n\nRegarding P-core (performance core) preference in Intel hybrid systems, as of kernel 4.10, Intel's ITMT (Intel Turbo Boost Max Technology) support caused the scheduler to prefer Pcores over Ecores, all else being equal. Pcores are faster; they run at a higher clock frequency, but are also able to get more work done with each clock cycle.\n\nThe search results focus primarily on Linux 6.6 changes but don't contain detailed information about specific changes in kernel 6.8 regarding this topic.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"1365c8b2-1efb-48c0-8b52-efffd378ecd9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:17.465Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ad0b02eb-0519-4e48-b0c1-25bb4d9f5b0f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"746946d7-500e-43f0-9b00-7eecef594da8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XNrJKAd2CFepcKz4epoAyw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Ubuntu 24.04 vs Arch vs Fedora kernel HPC compute performance benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Ubuntu 24.04 LTS & Fedora 40 Continue To Trail Intel's Linux Performance Optimizations - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/review/intel-ubuntu2404-fedora40\"},{\"title\":\"Clear Linux Vs. Ubuntu 24.04 Vs. Fedora 40 Performance Benchmarks - OpenBenchmarking.org\",\"url\":\"https://openbenchmarking.org/result/2405096-NE-INTELLINU24&sgm=1&swl&sor\"},{\"title\":\"12 Best Linux Distro for Performance in 2024 - LinuxSimply\",\"url\":\"https://linuxsimply.com/best-linux-distro-for-performance/\"},{\"title\":\"How to Benchmark Your Linux System\",\"url\":\"https://linuxconfig.org/how-to-benchmark-your-linux-system\"},{\"title\":\"Forbes report on linux performance - Fedora Discussion\",\"url\":\"https://discussion.fedoraproject.org/t/forbes-report-on-linux-performance/129257\"},{\"title\":\"Ubuntu vs Fedora [2026]: 7 Tests Reveal the Winner\",\"url\":\"https://tech-insider.org/ubuntu-vs-fedora-2026/\"},{\"title\":\"Phoronix - updated benchmarks - General Discussion - Clear Linux OS Forum\",\"url\":\"https://community.clearlinux.org/t/phoronix-updated-benchmarks/9647\"},{\"title\":\"What's the performance difference b/w arch linux and other distros like Ubuntu - Newbie - EndeavourOS\",\"url\":\"https://forum.endeavouros.com/t/whats-the-performance-difference-b-w-arch-linux-and-other-distros-like-ubuntu/47273\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding HPC and compute performance benchmarks for these distributions:\n\n## Ubuntu 24.04 vs Fedora vs Arch Performance\n\nWhile Ubuntu 24.04 LTS has received performance improvements, it's still not the fastest Linux distribution on x86_64 hardware, and Fedora Workstation 40 with its new GCC 14 compiler also has room for additional performance optimization.\n\nBenchmarks comparing Ubuntu 24.04 LTS and Fedora Workstation 40 show they compete with Intel's Clear Linux distribution, which offers aggressive x86_64 Linux performance defaults and the best possible out-of-the-box Linux performance on modern x86_64 hardware. Clear Linux continues to lead, though these other more notable Linux distributions have caught up in some areas.\n\n## Arch Linux References\n\nThe search results mention Arch Linux peripherally in relation to performance comparisons. There's a reference to CachyOS, an Arch Linux-based distribution, being evaluated for performance on AMD EPYC servers, but specific HPC compute performance benchmarks comparing vanilla Arch Linux directly with Ubuntu 24.04 and Fedora were not found in these results.\n\nFor more detailed HPC-specific benchmarks, you may want to check Phoronix's full review directly, as they appear to conduct comprehensive performance testing on multiple workloads.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"1fa2ca09-ae80-49ea-8937-5f47835176f3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:17.984Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"746946d7-500e-43f0-9b00-7eecef594da8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"d691d007-ea26-441f-b26b-bd37d741c14d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014xZMtT5wsHxdKJQvMMYP4X","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"perf stat dTLB-misses CP-SAT solver instruction count L3 cache miss\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Use perf to measure cache misses and TLB misses | CSCI 3150 - Memory Management I\",\"url\":\"https://millyz.github.io/ta/os3150_2016/mem1-lab/mem1/part1_2.html\"},{\"title\":\"Linux perf Examples\",\"url\":\"https://www.brendangregg.com/perf.html\"},{\"title\":\"Analyzing Cache Misses Using the perf Tool in Linux | Baeldung on Linux\",\"url\":\"https://www.baeldung.com/linux/analyze-cache-misses\"},{\"title\":\"perf-stat(1) - Linux manual page\",\"url\":\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/perf-stat.1.html\"},{\"title\":\"Use perf to Measure Cache Miss and TLB Miss | C program optimization\",\"url\":\"https://eric-lo.gitbook.io/program-optimization/measurement/use-perf-to-measure-cache-misses-and-tlb-misses\"},{\"title\":\"What's the easiest way to profile my application to see the CPU cache L1/L2 hit/miss ratio on Linux? - Quora\",\"url\":\"https://www.quora.com/Whats-the-easiest-way-to-profile-my-application-to-see-the-CPU-cache-L1-L2-hit-miss-ratio-on-Linux\"},{\"title\":\"PERF tutorial: Counting hardware performance events - Sand, software and soundSand, software and sound\",\"url\":\"https://sandsoftwaresound.net/perf/perf-tut-count-hw-events/\"},{\"title\":\"level 2 and 3 cache misses on Xeon E5 - Intel Community\",\"url\":\"https://community.intel.com/t5/Software-Tuning-Performance/level-2-and-3-cache-misses-on-Xeon-E5/td-p/921488\"},{\"title\":\"Measuring cache performance + perf - Joseph Muia\",\"url\":\"https://josephmuia.ca/2018-09-04-measuring-cache-performance-perf/\"},{\"title\":\"Using Performance Counters\",\"url\":\"https://relate.cs.illinois.edu/course/cs598apk-f18/f/demos/upload/perf/Using%20Performance%20Counters.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about using perf stat to measure dTLB-misses, instruction count, and L3 cache miss metrics:\n\n## Using perf stat for Performance Metrics\n\nYou can measure various CPU data TLB statistics using: `perf stat -e dTLB-loads,dTLB-load-misses,dTLB-prefetch-misses command`\n\nSimilarly, for CPU last level cache statistics, use: `perf stat -e LLC-loads,LLC-load-misses,LLC-stores,LLC-prefetches command`\n\n## Instruction Count Measurement\n\nThe instructions event enumerates the total number of instructions executed, providing insight into how efficiently the instructions execute (measured as instructions per cycle)\n\n## Detailed Event Tracking\n\nThe `-d` flag with perf stat offers detailed output: `-d -d` provides more detailed events including dTLB and iTLB events\n\n## L3/LLC Cache Misses\n\nLLC (last-level cache) metrics show misses that reached LLC (often L3) or memory\n\n## Regarding CP-SAT Solver\n\nThe search results do not contain specific information about using perf stat with the CP-SAT solver. If you're looking to profile CP-SAT (Google's Constraint Programming Solver) specifically, you would apply the standard perf commands to whatever CP-SAT executable or process you're running, but I couldn't find documentation that directly combines these performance metrics with CP-SAT benchmarking.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"56449796-66f8-4260-bb30-17be3a553859","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:19.126Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d691d007-ea26-441f-b26b-bd37d741c14d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
