{"parentUuid":"53fc917e-4db4-41d4-b185-eed24381435f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01472QZstNLwmvLH9LD2gHLt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Clear Linux Phoronix CP-SAT Z3 SAT solver compute benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Clear Linux - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/search/Clear+Linux\"},{\"title\":\"Phoronix - updated benchmarks - General Discussion - Clear Linux OS Forum\",\"url\":\"https://community.clearlinux.org/t/phoronix-updated-benchmarks/9647\"},{\"title\":\"phoronix-test-suite/ob-cache/openbenchmarking.org/pts.index at master · phoronix-test-suite/phoronix-test-suite\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/phoronix-test-suite/phoronix-test-suite/blob/master/ob-cache/openbenchmarking.org/pts.index\"},{\"title\":\"Z3 Theorem Prover Benchmark - OpenBenchmarking.org\",\"url\":\"https://openbenchmarking.org/test/pts/z3\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmark : Clear Linux vs The Rest - General Discussion - Clear Linux OS Forum\",\"url\":\"https://community.clearlinux.org/t/benchmark-clear-linux-vs-the-rest/7565\"},{\"title\":\"Linux Hardware Reviews, Performance Testing & Articles - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/scan.php?page=phoronix_articles\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Z3Prover/z3: The Z3 Theorem Prover · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3\"},{\"title\":\"CSC410 tutorial: solving SAT problems with Z3\",\"url\":\"http://www.cs.toronto.edu/~victorn/tutorials/z3_SAT_2019/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"Formal Veriﬁcation 2023-24: Practical Exercise 2 SAT & SMT with the Z3 solver 1\",\"url\":\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/fv/2023/ex2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"clear-linux-documentation/source/guides/clear/performance.rst at master · clearlinux/clear-linux-documentation\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/clearlinux/clear-linux-documentation/blob/master/source/guides/clear/performance.rst\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found:\n\n## Clear Linux and Phoronix Benchmarking\n\nPhoronix publishes Linux performance comparisons that include Clear Linux. Clear Linux uses many techniques in its software build toolchain to improve software performance, such as aggressive compiler flags and CPU-specific optimizations.\n\n## Z3 Theorem Prover Benchmark\n\nThe Z3 Theorem Prover / SMT solver is developed by Microsoft Research under the MIT license. This is available as a test in the Phoronix Test Suite. Based on OpenBenchmarking.org data, the selected test / test configuration (Z3 Theorem Prover 4.12.1 - SMT File: 2.smt2) has an average run-time of 4 minutes.\n\nHowever, the search results did not contain specific information about a \"CP-SAT\" compute benchmark in relation to Clear Linux and Z3. The results show general information about Clear Linux's performance optimization techniques, Phoronix's benchmarking work, and Z3 as a theorem prover/SAT solver, but don't specifically address the CP-SAT Z3 benchmark combination you're asking about.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0da8b1ea-23c8-4677-9d9f-d424dcf3e706","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:31.154Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"53fc917e-4db4-41d4-b185-eed24381435f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ca520b32-83c4-4816-8dd2-acca167131db","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011VYBsK8bdKh1EzK7KCniEG","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Linux glibc malloc tcmalloc jemalloc CP-SAT solver memory allocator performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Testing Memory Allocators: ptmalloc2 vs tcmalloc vs hoard vs jemalloc While Trying to Simulate Real-World Loads - IT Hare on Soft.ware\",\"url\":\"http://ithare.com/testing-memory-allocators-ptmalloc2-tcmalloc-hoard-jemalloc-while-trying-to-simulate-real-world-loads/\"},{\"title\":\"On the Impact of Memory Allocation on High-Performance Query Processing\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.01135\"},{\"title\":\"Small Datum: Battle of the Mallocators\",\"url\":\"http://smalldatum.blogspot.com/2025/04/battle-of-mallocators.html\"},{\"title\":\"Handling native memory fragmentation of glibc • Brice Dutheil\",\"url\":\"https://blog.arkey.fr/drafts/2021/01/22/native-memory-fragmentation-with-glibc/\"},{\"title\":\"libmalloc, jemalloc, tcmalloc, mimalloc - Exploring Different Memory Allocators - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/frosnerd/libmalloc-jemalloc-tcmalloc-mimalloc-exploring-different-memory-allocators-4lp3\"},{\"title\":\"Improve MySQL and MariaDB performance with memory allocators like Jemalloc and TCMalloc - Managed Server\",\"url\":\"https://www.managedserver.eu/Improve-mysql-and-mariadb-performance-with-memory-allocators-like-jemalloc-and-tcmalloc/\"},{\"title\":\"Very high virtual memory consumption and slow performance · Issue #285 · google/tcmalloc\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/tcmalloc/issues/285\"},{\"title\":\"Reducing the use of non-glibc allocators in Fedora [LWN.net]\",\"url\":\"https://lwn.net/Articles/761502/\"},{\"title\":\"Testing Memory Allocators: ptmalloc2 vs. tcmalloc vs. hoard vs. jemalloc | Hacker News\",\"url\":\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=17457699\"},{\"title\":\"jemalloc vs glibc malloc: Memory Allocation Performance Comparison | by Binita Bharati | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@binitabharati/jemalloc-vs-glibc-malloc-memory-allocation-performance-comparison-fbaeda1740de\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for information about Linux memory allocators (glibc malloc, tcmalloc, jemalloc) and their performance characteristics. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Performance Characteristics\n\n**For Multi-threaded Workloads:**\nJemalloc and TCMalloc have maintained stable increases in throughput up to 6300 TPS with up to 4096 threads, while glibc malloc throughput dropped dramatically after 16 threads.\n\n**Memory Overhead:**\nFor MyRocks (RocksDB), peak RSS is smallest with jemalloc (1.22x), slightly larger with tcmalloc (1.31x), and much larger with glibc malloc (3.62x) compared to the buffer pool size.\n\n**Comparative Advantages:**\n- If your database has memory fragmentation problems, Jemalloc is the best choice\n- If you need to reduce latency in multi-threaded workloads, TCMalloc can offer superior benefits\n\n**Design Differences:**\nJemalloc maintains a cache per thread, while tcmalloc maintains a pool of caches with threads developing affinity for a cache but able to change.\n\n**Modern Glibc Improvements:**\nGlibc 2.26 introduced a newer, much more scalable implementation of malloc with a per-thread cache, though it was still worse than tcmalloc/jemalloc in almost every aspect in some benchmarks.\n\n**Key Recommendation:**\nIf the server has more than 8 cores, it is advisable to benchmark with Jemalloc or TCMalloc, as they can significantly improve performance at no additional cost.\n\nNote: The search results did not contain specific information about CP-SAT solver memory allocator performance.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"12e9f94b-4630-45b1-a5e7-14965dd9440f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:34.330Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ca520b32-83c4-4816-8dd2-acca167131db","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6166dd08-8145-464b-8b40-9c3ad93fd356","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"8dfd7d49-883e-40c9-8948-d7864c6a5fee","agentId":"a8a152668dd067210","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzHQ6pYL7pczpn3krxm1VS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"intel-thread-director\" Linux 6.8 6.10 P-core E-core scheduling improvements\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Intel's Thread Director Coming to Linux 5.18 to Fix Alder Lake Performance Issues | Tom's Hardware\",\"url\":\"https://www.tomshardware.com/news/intel-thread-director-coming-to-linux-5-18\"},{\"title\":\"Intel Thread Director Is Headed to Linux for a Major Boost in Alder Lake Performance - Slashdot\",\"url\":\"https://linux.slashdot.org/story/22/02/12/2237234/intel-thread-director-is-headed-to-linux-for-a-major-boost-in-alder-lake-performance\"},{\"title\":\"Take control of your Intel CPU's P-Cores and E-Cores with CoreDirector software | Tom's Hardware\",\"url\":\"https://www.tomshardware.com/news/bitsun-coredirector-intel-cpu-e-cores\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of the Intel Thread Director technology on an Alder Lake processor\",\"url\":\"https://pmctrack-linux.github.io/assets/papers/apsys22-saez.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Flexible system software scheduling for asymmetric multicore systems with PMCSched: A case for Intel Alder Lake - Bilbao - 2023 - Concurrency and Computation: Practice and Experience - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/cpe.7814\"},{\"title\":\"The current state of Alder Lake E-cores and P-cores on Linux - Linux - Level1Techs Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forum.level1techs.com/t/the-current-state-of-alder-lake-e-cores-and-p-cores-on-linux/199603\"},{\"title\":\"Intel Thread Director Is Headed To Linux For A Major Boost In Alder Lake Performance | HotHardware\",\"url\":\"https://hothardware.com/news/intel-thread-director-linux-boost-alder-lake-performance\"},{\"title\":\"Intel's Alder Lake is slowing down Linux kernel 5.16 • The Register\",\"url\":\"https://www.theregister.com/2021/12/01/linux_5_16_alder_lake/\"},{\"title\":\"Hybrid scheduling gets more complicated [LWN.net]\",\"url\":\"https://lwn.net/Articles/909611/\"},{\"title\":\"Intel Alder Lake's Thread Director Support Coming to Linux | Tom's Hardware\",\"url\":\"https://www.tomshardware.com/news/intel-alder-lake-thread-director-support-coming-to-linux\"}]\n\nLet me search for more specific information about Linux 6.8 and 6.10 kernel improvements:\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Intel Posts New Linux Patches For Cluster Scheduling With Hybrid CPUs - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Hybrid-CPU-Cluster-Sched\"},{\"title\":\"EEVDF Scheduler Merged For Linux 6.6, Intel Hybrid Cluster Scheduling Re-Introduced - Phoronix Forums\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/forums/forum/phoronix/latest-phoronix-articles/1406196-eevdf-scheduler-merged-for-linux-6-6-intel-hybrid-cluster-scheduling-re-introduced\"},{\"title\":\"Intel Updates Cache Aware Scheduling for Linux 2026: Performance Boost for Intel & AMD CPUs - Tech Edu Byte\",\"url\":\"https://www.techedubyte.com/intel-cache-aware-scheduling-linux-2026-performance-boost-cp-2/\"},{\"title\":\"Linux 6.18 Kernel: Intel Hybrid Optimizations Boost Performance\",\"url\":\"https://www.webpronews.com/linux-6-18-kernel-intel-hybrid-optimizations-boost-performance/\"},{\"title\":\"Intel 2026 Cache Aware Scheduling Update for Linux: Enhanced CPU Performance with Multi-Cache Support - Tech Edu Byte\",\"url\":\"https://www.techedubyte.com/intel-2026-cache-aware-scheduling-linux-cpu-performance/\"},{\"title\":\"Hybrid scheduling gets more complicated [LWN.net]\",\"url\":\"https://lwn.net/Articles/909611/\"},{\"title\":\"intel_pstate CPU Performance Scaling Driver — The Linux Kernel documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/intel_pstate.html\"},{\"title\":\"14. Hardware-Feedback Interface for scheduling on Intel Hardware — The Linux Kernel documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.kernel.org/arch/x86/intel-hfi.html\"},{\"title\":\"Intel Updates x86 Hybrid CPU Cluster Scheduling For The Linux Kernel – Lifeboat News: The Blog\",\"url\":\"https://lifeboat.com/blog/2023/07/intel-updates-x86-hybrid-cpu-cluster-scheduling-for-the-linux-kernel\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's information about Intel Thread Director, Linux P-core and E-core scheduling improvements, particularly relevant to kernel versions around 6.8 and 6.10:\n\n## Overview of Intel Thread Director and Scheduling\n\nIntel's \"Thread Director\" is an intelligent, low-latency hardware-assisted scheduler that was designed to optimize task placement on Intel's hybrid processors combining performance and efficiency cores.\n\n## Early Support (Linux 5.18 onwards)\n\nLinux version 5.18 improved the performance of Intel's 12th Generation Core 'Alder Lake' processors with drivers that optimize usage of (P)erformance and (E)fficiency cores. Unlike Windows 11, Linux did not have proper support for Intel's Thread Director technology based on the Enhanced Hardware Feedback Interface (HFI) initially.\n\n## How Thread Director Works\n\nIntel's Thread Director communicates actual numeric performance and numeric power efficiency values of each CPU core in a 0 – 255 range to the OS. The idea is that background tasks and light workloads can be run on the E-cores, saving power, while latency-sensitive and compute-intensive tasks can be run on the faster P-cores.\n\n## Recent Improvements (Linux 6.6 and Beyond)\n\nThe EEVDF scheduler code was merged for Linux 6.6, and this scheduler pull re-introduced cluster scheduling for Intel Core hybrid processors.\n\n## Linux 6.10 Cache-Aware Scheduling Update\n\nThe Intel cache aware scheduling update will be available in Linux kernel version 6.10, scheduled for release in the second quarter of 2026, and will support all Intel processors from the 12th generation Alder Lake onwards.\n\n## Key Improvements\n\nImproved cache topology detection allows the kernel to better identify the cache structure of both Intel and AMD CPUs for more precise task placement, with enhanced load balancing distributing tasks more intelligently across cores.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c6a3b146-5f68-453e-af38-4b017908774c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:32:36.133Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6166dd08-8145-464b-8b40-9c3ad93fd356","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
